// Medical Innovation lessons 6–10: impact on health care delivery and quality of life, costs and value,
// training, careers and implementation, the digital outline and exhibit, and the Round Two presentation.
const RULES=['HOSA Medical Innovation 2026–27 guidelines and rating sheets','https://hosa.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/MI-26-27-9.25.pdf'];
const FDA=['FDA: overview of device regulation','https://www.fda.gov/medical-devices/device-advice-comprehensive-regulatory-assistance/overview-device-regulation'];
const CLASSIFY=['FDA: classify your medical device','https://www.fda.gov/medical-devices/overview-device-regulation/classify-your-medical-device'];
const NHE=['CMS: National Health Expenditure data','https://www.cms.gov/data-research/statistics-trends-and-reports/national-health-expenditure-data'];
const HP=['ODPHP: Healthy People 2030','https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople'];
const BLS_OOH=['U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics: Occupational Outlook Handbook—healthcare occupations','https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/home.htm'];
const UPLOAD=['HOSA: Competitive event digital uploads','https://hosa.org/competitive-event-digital-uploads/'];
const q=(p,a,b,c,d,e)=>[p,[a,b,c,d],e];
const match=(title,pairs)=>({t:'w',kind:'match',title,pairs});
const order=(title,steps)=>({t:'w',kind:'order',title,steps});
const sort=(title,buckets,items)=>({t:'w',kind:'sort',title,buckets,items});
const flip=(title,cards)=>({t:'w',kind:'flip',title,cards});
const kase=(title,nodes)=>({t:'w',kind:'case',title,start:'a',nodes});
const chk=(q,options,why)=>({t:'check',q,options,why});
const ex=(title,prompt,steps,answer)=>({t:'example',title,prompt,steps,answer});

export const MI6={title:'Impact on health care delivery and quality of life',hook:'Two of the highest-value Round One items ask the same big question from different angles: How would your innovation change health care, and how would it change people’s lives? Judges award up to 15 points each for comprehensive, clear explanations. Strong answers connect your prototype to real outcomes for patients, families and health workers.',objectives:['Explain how an innovation can change health care delivery.','Describe quality-of-life impacts across physical, emotional and social domains.','Support impact claims with evidence and realistic reasoning.','Present impact clearly within slide limits.'],sections:[
['What “health care delivery” means',[
'**Health care delivery** is how care reaches people: who provides it, where, when and how efficiently. Innovations can change delivery by **moving care** to new settings (from hospital to home, from clinic to telehealth), **improving safety** (fewer errors, infections or falls), **improving efficiency** (less time searching, fewer repeated tests), **improving access** (rural, low-income or disabled populations) and **supporting the workforce** (reducing burnout or physical strain).',
'Think about the **future**: how might care look different five or ten years from now if your innovation were widely used?',
'The outline allows **up to two slides** for impact on the future of health care delivery.',
"Consider also how your innovation fits larger trends in health care, such as the growth of home-based care, remote monitoring, preventive care and an aging population. Connecting to these trends strengthens claims about the future.",
sort('Type of delivery impact',['Safety','Efficiency','Access'],[['Fewer medication errors',0],['Less time charting',1],['Care available in rural homes',2],['Fewer hospital-acquired infections',0],['Faster triage in emergency rooms',1],['Wheelchair-accessible monitoring',2]])]],
['Outcomes that matter',[
'Describe impact in terms of **outcomes**: fewer injuries or complications, shorter hospital stays, fewer readmissions, earlier detection, faster treatment, better adherence to medicines or therapy and improved patient satisfaction. For health workers, outcomes include time saved, less physical strain and fewer alarms.',
'Be specific: “could reduce nighttime falls” is stronger with a mechanism—“by alerting staff before residents stand and lighting the path.” Link each outcome to how the innovation works.',
'Use language such as “could,” “may” or “is designed to,” because your prototype has not been clinically proven.',
"If possible, describe how you would measure the outcomes in a real pilot—for example, comparing fall rates before and after use. This shows judges how impact could be proven over time.",
chk('Which claim is most appropriate for a student prototype?',['“Is designed to reduce nighttime falls by alerting staff before residents stand.”','“Will eliminate all falls.”','“Is proven to save every patient.”','“Has no risks at all.”'],'Realistic, mechanism-based claims.')]],
['Quality of life',[
'**Quality of life** includes physical health, emotional well-being, independence, social connection and the ability to do meaningful daily activities. Health-related quality of life is a focus of national goals such as **Healthy People 2030**.',
'Describe impacts for **patients** (less pain, more independence, confidence, dignity, sleep), **caregivers** (less worry, fewer nighttime checks) and **health workers** (less stress, more time with patients). Personal stories from interviews make these impacts vivid.',
'The outline allows **up to two slides** for quality of life.',
"Quality-of-life impacts are often what people remember. A short story about a resident who wants to remain independent can make the impact personal and memorable for judges.",
match('Quality-of-life domain and example',[['Physical','Fewer injuries and less pain'],['Emotional','Less fear of falling'],['Independence','Walking to the bathroom safely alone'],['Social','Joining activities with confidence'],['Sleep','Fewer disruptive alarms at night'],['Caregiver','Less worry for families']])]],
['Supporting impact with evidence',[
'Ground impact claims in **evidence**: statistics on the problem’s size and consequences, research on similar interventions, expert opinions from interviews and your own **test results**. For example, research showing that improved lighting reduces falls supports your path-lighting feature.',
'Show **reasoning**: “If falls cost facilities about X each and our device prevents even a small fraction, the impact could be…” Label any estimates clearly as estimates and cite sources.',
'Accurate, current information presented logically earns top scores.',
"Distinguish clearly between what your tests showed and what you predict. “In our trials, alerts came within 4 seconds” is a result; “this could reduce falls” is a prediction based on evidence.",
ex('Building an impact argument','Construct an impact chain for a fall-prevention device (numbers invented for practice).',['Problem: a facility has about 40 resident falls per year, many at night','Mechanism: early alerts and path lighting','Evidence: research links better lighting and early assistance with fewer falls','Estimate: preventing 25% would avoid about 10 falls per year','Outcomes: fewer injuries, more confidence and less staff stress'],'A clear chain from problem to mechanism, evidence and outcomes (numbers invented for practice).')]],
['Considering limitations and equity',[
'Strong presentations acknowledge **limitations**: What populations or settings might it not work for? What could reduce its effectiveness? Addressing limitations shows judges deep understanding and honesty.',
'Consider **equity**: Will people with low incomes, disabilities, limited technology access or different languages benefit? Designing for affordability and accessibility can increase impact across populations.',
"Addressing limitations can also point to future improvements. Explaining how a next version might serve residents with vision loss shows that your team thinks beyond the current prototype.",
sort('Strengthens or weakens an impact explanation?',['Strengthens','Weakens'],[['Acknowledging that it needs staff to respond to alerts',0],['Claiming it works for everyone everywhere',1],['Explaining a low-cost version for small facilities',0],['Ignoring residents who cannot see lights well',1],['Citing research on similar approaches',0]])]],
['Presenting impact visually',[
'On impact slides, use **before-and-after** comparisons, simple **flow diagrams** of care with and without your innovation, **icons** for outcomes and short quotes. Keep text concise and focus on two or three major impacts per slide.',
'In the exhibit, an infographic panel showing “Today” versus “With our innovation” helps viewers understand the impact without a presenter.',
"Keep impact slides focused on two or three of your strongest points. A few well-supported impacts are more convincing than a long list of possible benefits.",
order('A before-and-after care flow (example)',['Today: resident gets up alone in the dark','Today: fall risk and delayed discovery','With innovation: sensor detects sitting up','With innovation: lights guide the way','With innovation: staff arrive to assist','Outcome: safer, more confident resident'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Impact slide review',{a:{text:'Your impact slide says only, “This will help hospitals.” (Scenario invented for practice.) How do you strengthen it?',choices:[['Name specific delivery changes, outcomes, mechanisms and evidence','b','Correct.',true],['Add a bigger font','b','Content is the issue.',false]]},b:{text:'A judge might ask who your innovation does not help. How do you prepare?',choices:[['Identify limitations and equity considerations honestly','c','Correct.',true],['Claim it helps everyone','c','Acknowledging limits builds credibility.',false]]},c:{text:'Your impact slides are specific, evidence-based and honest.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Health care delivery impacts include moving care to new settings, safety, efficiency, access and workforce support; describe the future of care if the innovation were widely used (up to 2 slides).','Quality of life covers physical, emotional, independence, social and caregiver impacts (up to 2 slides); describe outcomes with mechanisms and use realistic language such as “designed to” or “could.”','Support claims with statistics, research, expert input and test results; show reasoning with labeled estimates; acknowledge limitations and equity; present impact with before-and-after visuals.'],terms:[['Health care delivery','How care reaches people: who, where, when and how.'],['Quality of life','Physical, emotional and social well-being and the ability to do daily activities.'],['Outcome','A measurable result of care, such as fewer injuries.']],mcqs:[q('How many slides are allowed for impact on health care delivery?','Up to 2','Up to 4','1','Unlimited','Outline.'),q('Moving care from hospital to home is an example of:','A change in health care delivery','A trademark','A patent claim','A constraint','Delivery.'),q('Which is a quality-of-life impact?','Less fear of falling','Lower shipping cost','Larger font','More slides','Emotional.'),q('Which claim is most appropriate?','“Is designed to reduce falls”','“Will eliminate all falls”','“Is proven perfect”','“Has no limitations”','Realistic.'),q('Why acknowledge limitations?','It shows deep understanding and honesty','It lowers your score automatically','It is required by patent law','It is never useful','Credibility.'),q('Which supports an impact claim best?','Research on similar interventions','A guess','A slogan','A color scheme','Evidence.'),q('Equity considerations ask whether:','Diverse populations can benefit','The exhibit is tall','The slides are blue','The patent is filed','Access.'),q('Which visual best shows impact?','A before-and-after care flow','A wall of text','A blank slide','An unrelated photo','Comparison.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how your team would describe your innovation’s impact on the future of health care delivery and on quality of life.',rubric:[['Delivery impact',['delivery','setting','safety','efficiency','access','workforce','future']],['Quality of life',['quality of life','physical','emotional','independence','caregivers','staff']],['Evidence and reasoning',['evidence','research','statistics','test results','estimate','mechanism']],['Limits and presentation',['limitations','equity','before-and-after','flow','concise','designed to']]],model:'For our fall-prevention system, the impact on health care delivery would center on safety, efficiency and workforce support. Today, residents in nursing homes often get up alone in the dark, and falls may not be discovered quickly; with our innovation, a sensor detects a resident sitting up, soft lights guide the way and staff receive a silent alert so they can arrive before the resident stands. In the future, facilities using such systems could shift from reacting to falls to preventing them, reduce the time nurses spend on frequent room checks and lower alarm fatigue compared with loud bed alarms. For quality of life, residents could gain independence and confidence walking to the bathroom, experience fewer injuries and less fear of falling and sleep better without loud alarms, while families worry less and staff feel less stress. We would support these claims with CDC data on the frequency and consequences of falls among older adults, research linking better lighting and timely assistance to fewer falls, quotes from nurses we interviewed and our own test results showing alerts within 5 seconds in 90% of trials. We would show our reasoning with clearly labeled estimates and use realistic language such as “is designed to” and “could reduce.” We would acknowledge limitations—it requires staff to respond and may need adaptation for residents with severe vision loss—and consider equity through a low-cost version for small facilities. On two slides each, we would use before-and-after care flows, outcome icons and short quotes.'},sources:[RULES,HP]};

export const MI7={title:'Costs, savings and value',hook:'Health care in the United States costs trillions of dollars each year, and every new product must answer a hard question: Is it worth it? Round One awards up to 15 points for explaining how your innovation may reduce health care costs, and Round Two asks for the anticipated cost to consumers or the health care system. Clear, honest numbers make your innovation credible.',objectives:['Estimate the cost of producing and using an innovation.','Explain direct and indirect ways an innovation can reduce costs.','Perform a simple cost-benefit comparison.','Present cost information clearly and honestly.'],sections:[
['Why cost matters',[
'National health spending in the United States is very large, and hospitals, insurers, governments and families all face pressure to control costs. Innovations that **improve outcomes while lowering costs**—or that deliver more value for the same cost—are more likely to be adopted.',
'Judges look for an exceptional description of how the innovation reduces health care costs (Round One) and detailed information about the **cost of the innovation for the consumer and/or the health care system** (Round Two).',
'The outline allows **up to two slides** for reducing health care costs.',
"Costs matter to patients as well. High out-of-pocket costs can prevent people from using helpful technologies, so an affordable design can improve both access and outcomes.",
"Cost savings can be described for different groups: patients, families, facilities, insurers and society. Choosing the groups most affected by your innovation keeps the explanation focused.",
flip('Cost questions to answer',[['What does it cost to make?','Materials and manufacturing'],['What will buyers pay?','Estimated price'],['Who pays?','Consumer, facility or insurer'],['What costs does it reduce?','Injuries, stays, labor, errors'],['When does it pay off?','Break-even point'],['Are there ongoing costs?','Batteries, maintenance, training']])]],
['Estimating the cost of your innovation',[
'Start with a **bill of materials**: list each component and its price. Add estimated **manufacturing**, **packaging**, **software** and **maintenance** costs. Your prototype cost is a starting point; mass production often lowers per-unit cost, while regulatory testing and support add costs.',
'Distinguish **one-time costs** (purchase, installation) from **ongoing costs** (batteries, replacement parts, subscriptions, training).',
'Label estimates clearly and explain assumptions.',
"Research typical costs of existing solutions as part of your estimate. Knowing what facilities already pay for similar products helps you set a realistic price and explain why yours offers better value.",
ex('Bill of materials','Estimate the materials cost of one prototype unit (prices invented for practice).',['Pressure sensor pad: $12','Microcontroller: $8','LED light strip: $6','Battery pack: $5','3D-printed housing: $4','Total materials: $35 per unit (before labor and testing)'],'About $35 in materials per unit (prices invented for practice).')]],
['How innovations reduce costs',[
'**Direct savings** include preventing expensive events (falls, infections, readmissions), reducing supply waste, shortening hospital stays and avoiding repeated tests. **Indirect savings** include saving staff time, reducing staff injuries and turnover, improving productivity and reducing missed work for patients and caregivers.',
'Some innovations shift care to **lower-cost settings**, such as home monitoring instead of hospital stays, or enable **earlier detection**, when treatment is often less expensive.',
'Identify which savings apply to your innovation and who benefits from them.',
"Some innovations increase costs at first but reduce them over time. Explain the time frame clearly: a device that costs money to install might save more over a year by preventing expensive events.",
sort('Direct or indirect savings?',['Direct','Indirect'],[['Fewer fall-related hospitalizations',0],['Less nurse time spent on room checks',1],['Fewer repeated lab tests',0],['Lower staff injury and turnover',1],['Shorter hospital stays',0],['Caregivers missing less work',1]])]],
['A simple cost-benefit comparison',[
'Compare the **total cost** of using the innovation with the **costs it avoids**. If the avoided costs are greater, the innovation saves money. The **break-even point** is when cumulative savings equal the investment.',
'Use published cost data for the problem (for example, average costs of a fall injury or a hospital-acquired infection) and conservative assumptions about effectiveness.',
"Use conservative assumptions to make your estimate credible. If you show savings are positive even when the innovation prevents only a small share of events, judges are more likely to trust your reasoning.",
ex('Break-even estimate','A facility buys 20 units at $150 each (figures invented for practice). Each prevented fall avoids about $10,000 in costs. How many prevented falls pay for the purchase?',['Investment: 20 × $150 = $3,000','Savings per prevented fall: $10,000','Break-even: $3,000 ÷ $10,000 = 0.3 falls','Preventing even one fall more than covers the purchase'],'Less than one prevented fall covers the cost (figures invented for practice).')]],
['Who pays and pricing',[
'Identify the **purchaser**: a consumer buying for home use, a hospital or nursing home, or an insurer or government program that might cover it. Each has different priorities—consumers care about price and ease, facilities about total cost and staff time and insurers about avoided claims.',
'Compare your estimated price with **existing solutions** from your comparison table. A lower price, or a higher price justified by better outcomes, should be explained clearly.',
'Consider affordability for low-income users or small facilities.',
"Insurance coverage and reimbursement can strongly affect adoption. If insurers or government programs pay for similar devices, mention it; if not, explain who would pay and why.",
match('Purchaser and main priority',[['Consumer','Affordable price and ease of use'],['Hospital','Total cost and outcomes'],['Nursing home','Staff time and resident safety'],['Insurer','Fewer costly claims'],['Government program','Population outcomes and budget'],['Clinic','Workflow efficiency']])]],
['Presenting costs honestly',[
'Show costs in a simple **table** (unit cost, ongoing costs, estimated price) and savings in a **comparison chart**. State assumptions and sources. Avoid overstating savings—judges trust conservative, well-reasoned estimates.',
'In Round Two, be ready to explain anticipated costs to the consumer or health care system in a few clear sentences.',
"Practice explaining your cost estimate aloud in under 20 seconds. In Round Two, a short, confident summary leaves more time for the demonstration while still earning the cost points.",
chk('Which cost statement is most credible?',['“Using published fall-cost data and a conservative 10% reduction, a facility could save about…”','“It will save billions guaranteed.”','“It costs nothing.”','“Costs do not matter.”'],'Conservative, sourced estimates.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Cost questions',{a:{text:'A judge asks, “Who would pay for your device?” (Scenario invented for practice.) What is a strong answer?',choices:[['Nursing homes would purchase it, because preventing falls reduces injury costs and staff time','b','Correct.',true],['“We don’t know.”','b','Identify the purchaser and their motivation.',false]]},b:{text:'Your prototype cost $90, but you claim it will sell for $10. How do you justify the gap?',choices:[['Explain mass-production savings with a realistic estimate and assumptions','c','Correct.',true],['Avoid the question','c','Explain assumptions honestly.',false]]},c:{text:'Your cost explanation is clear and credible.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Cost matters to adoption; the outline allows up to two slides for reducing health care costs, and Round Two asks for anticipated costs to consumers or the health care system.','Estimate costs with a bill of materials plus manufacturing, software, maintenance and training; separate one-time and ongoing costs; describe direct (prevented events, shorter stays) and indirect (staff time, turnover) savings.','Use simple cost-benefit and break-even estimates with published cost data and conservative assumptions; identify the purchaser and compare prices with existing solutions; present labeled, sourced estimates.'],terms:[['Bill of materials','A list of components and their costs.'],['Break-even point','When cumulative savings equal the investment.'],['Direct savings','Costs avoided by preventing events such as injuries.']],mcqs:[q('How many outline slides are allowed for reducing health care costs?','Up to 2','Up to 4','1','None','Outline.'),q('A bill of materials lists:','Components and their costs','Team members','References','Judges','Cost estimate.'),q('Which is a direct saving?','Fewer fall-related hospitalizations','Less staff turnover','Better morale','Caregiver productivity','Prevented events.'),q('Which is an indirect saving?','Less nurse time on room checks','Fewer hospitalizations','Fewer repeated tests','Shorter stays','Time.'),q('The break-even point is when:','Savings equal the investment','The prototype breaks','The team stops working','Costs double','Payback.'),q('Which cost statement is most credible?','A conservative, sourced estimate','A guarantee of billions','“It costs nothing.”','No numbers at all','Honesty.'),q('Which is an ongoing cost?','Replacement batteries','Initial purchase','Design sketches','Patent search','Recurring.'),q('Who is often the purchaser for a nursing home device?','The nursing home','The judges','The designer','The patent office','Buyer.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how your team would estimate your innovation’s cost and show how it may reduce health care costs.',rubric:[['Cost of innovation',['bill of materials','components','manufacturing','one-time','ongoing','estimate']],['Savings',['direct','indirect','prevented','hospital','staff time','lower-cost setting']],['Analysis',['cost-benefit','break-even','published','conservative','assumptions']],['Purchaser and presentation',['purchaser','consumer','facility','insurer','price','compare','table']]],model:'We would start with a bill of materials for our fall-prevention system—sensor pad, microcontroller, LED light strip, battery pack and 3D-printed housing—totaling about $35 per unit in our prototype, then estimate manufacturing, packaging, software, maintenance and support to set a realistic price of about $150 per unit, clearly labeling these as estimates and noting that mass production could lower component costs. We would separate one-time costs, such as purchase and setup, from ongoing costs, such as batteries and staff training. To show how the innovation may reduce health care costs, we would describe direct savings from preventing fall injuries, emergency visits and hospital stays, and indirect savings from less nurse time spent on frequent room checks, fewer staff injuries from responding to falls and reduced alarm fatigue. Using published data on the average cost of fall injuries among older adults and a conservative assumption that the system prevents a small share of nighttime falls, we would calculate a break-even point; for example, if a facility buys 20 units for $3,000 and each prevented fall avoids about $10,000, preventing a single fall more than pays for the purchase. We would identify nursing homes as the main purchaser, explain that their priorities are resident safety and staff time and compare our price with existing bed alarms and pressure mats from our comparison table. Finally, we would present costs in a simple table and savings in a chart with sources and assumptions listed.'},sources:[RULES,NHE]};

export const MI8={title:'Training, careers and implementation',hook:'An innovation is only useful if people can use it correctly and if it fits into real health care. Round Two asks teams to describe the training required to use or implement the innovation and how it fits within the health care field—which practitioners and consumers are needed. Thinking about implementation shows judges that your idea could work in the real world.',objectives:['Describe training requirements for users and implementers.','Identify health careers involved in using and supporting an innovation.','Explain basic regulatory pathways for medical devices.','Plan realistic implementation steps.'],sections:[
['Training requirements',[
'Every innovation needs some training. Describe **who** needs training (patients, caregivers, nurses, technicians, IT staff), **what** they must learn (setup, use, cleaning, troubleshooting, responding to alerts), **how** training is delivered (in-person demonstration, video, quick-start guide, simulation) and **how long** it takes.',
'Simple innovations may need only a short demonstration; complex devices may need certification or competency checks. Easier training increases adoption.',
'Judges award full points for a detailed description of training requirements.',
"Include training materials in your exhibit if possible, such as a sample quick-start card. Showing a real training tool demonstrates that you have thought about how the innovation would be used day to day.",
"Consider training for patients and families too. Clear instructions in plain language, with pictures, help people use home-based innovations safely and confidently.",
match('Training element and example',[['Who','Night-shift nursing assistants'],['What','Placing the pad and responding to alerts'],['How','Ten-minute demonstration and quick-start card'],['How long','About 15 minutes per staff member'],['Competency check','Staff demonstrate setup correctly'],['Ongoing','Annual refresher and new-hire training']])]],
['Careers and the health care team',[
'Identify the **practitioners and consumers** needed to implement your innovation. For a fall-prevention system: **nursing assistants** and **nurses** respond to alerts, **nurse managers** set policies, **biomedical equipment technicians** install and maintain devices, **IT staff** manage alerts, **physical therapists** assess fall risk and **residents and families** use and support it.',
'Also consider careers involved in **creating** the innovation: biomedical engineers, product designers, software developers, clinical researchers, regulatory affairs specialists and sales and marketing professionals.',
'Clear career connections earn points for career implications.',
"Interviewing a professional in one of these roles strengthens this section. A biomedical technician, for instance, can tell you what makes devices easy or hard to maintain.",
sort('Creating or using the innovation?',['Creating it','Using or supporting it'],[['Biomedical engineer',0],['Nursing assistant',1],['Regulatory affairs specialist',0],['Biomedical equipment technician',1],['Clinical research coordinator',0],['Physical therapist assessing fall risk',1]])]],
['Regulation of medical devices',[
'In the United States, the **FDA** regulates medical devices based on risk. **Class I** devices (such as bandages and tongue depressors) are low risk and mostly need general controls. **Class II** devices (such as many monitoring devices) are moderate risk and often require **premarket notification (510(k))** showing they are substantially equivalent to an existing device. **Class III** devices (such as implantable pacemakers) are high risk and usually require **premarket approval (PMA)** with clinical evidence.',
'Some software can also be regulated as a medical device. Mentioning the likely class and pathway shows realistic planning.',
'You do not need FDA approval to compete, but understanding it strengthens your presentation.',
"Regulation also includes other requirements, such as electrical safety standards, data privacy laws for apps that handle health information and infection control standards for devices that touch patients.",
sort('Likely FDA device class?',['Class I (low risk)','Class II (moderate risk)','Class III (high risk)'],[['Elastic bandage',0],['Bedside monitoring sensor',1],['Implantable pacemaker',2],['Tongue depressor',0],['Powered wheelchair',1],['Implanted heart valve',2]])]],
['Steps to implementation',[
'Real-world implementation might include: **refining the design**, **testing** for safety and effectiveness, **regulatory clearance**, **manufacturing**, a **pilot** in a few facilities, **training staff**, **evaluating results** and **scaling up**. Partnerships with hospitals, universities or companies often help innovators move forward.',
'Describe a realistic **next step** for your team or for a future developer, such as consulting engineers or piloting with a local facility.',
"Clinical studies are often part of implementation, especially for higher-risk devices. These studies test whether the innovation is safe and effective in real patients under careful supervision and ethical review.",
order('From prototype to practice',['Refine the design','Test safety and effectiveness','Obtain regulatory clearance','Manufacture units','Pilot in a few facilities','Train staff and evaluate results','Scale up'])]],
['Barriers to adoption',[
'Even good innovations face barriers: **cost**, **workflow disruption**, **training time**, **resistance to change**, **integration** with existing systems (such as electronic health records), **privacy and security** for data and **reimbursement** (whether insurers pay).',
'Explain how your design reduces barriers—simple setup, low cost, compatibility with existing equipment and minimal training.',
'Anticipating barriers shows judges a thoughtful, practical team.',
"Listen for barriers during interviews. Health workers often mention practical concerns—such as cleaning time or battery changes—that can be addressed in the design before they become reasons not to adopt it.",
chk('Which design choice reduces a barrier to adoption?',['Making setup take only a few steps with no new software','Requiring a new computer system','Needing a week of training','Doubling the price'],'Simplicity increases adoption.')]],
['Fitting into the health care field',[
'Describe where your innovation fits: the **settings** (nursing homes, hospitals, homes), **specialties** (geriatrics, rehabilitation), the **existing workflows** it supports and how it complements other tools. Show that it solves a problem practitioners recognize.',
'Interviews with professionals provide powerful evidence: “The nurse manager we interviewed said this would fit into their night rounds.”',
"Describe how the innovation would be supported after purchase, including who answers questions, replaces parts and updates software. Ongoing support is part of real-world implementation.",
ex('Career implications summary','Summarize career implications for a fall-prevention system (details invented for practice).',['Users: nursing assistants and nurses respond to alerts','Support: biomedical technicians install and maintain; IT manages alerts','Clinical: physical therapists identify high-risk residents','Leaders: nurse managers set policies','Creators: engineers and designers refine the product'],'A clear map of who uses, supports and creates the innovation (details invented for practice).')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Implementation questions',{a:{text:'A judge asks, “How would nurses learn to use this?” (Scenario invented for practice.) What is a strong answer?',choices:[['A 10-minute demonstration, a quick-start card and a competency check, with refresher training for new hires','b','Correct.',true],['“They would figure it out.”','b','Describe training specifically.',false]]},b:{text:'The judge asks whether the device needs FDA review. What do you say?',choices:[['It would likely be a Class II device requiring premarket notification, and we would research the exact pathway','c','Correct.',true],['“Medical devices are not regulated.”','c','The FDA regulates devices by risk.',false]]},c:{text:'Your answers show realistic implementation planning.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Describe training: who, what (setup, use, cleaning, alerts), how (demonstration, video, quick-start card, simulation), how long and competency checks.','Identify careers that use and support the innovation (nurses, assistants, technicians, IT, therapists) and that create it (engineers, designers, researchers, regulatory specialists).','The FDA regulates devices by risk (Class I low, II moderate—often 510(k), III high—usually PMA); plan implementation steps, anticipate barriers (cost, workflow, training, integration, privacy, reimbursement) and show fit with real workflows.'],terms:[['510(k)','FDA premarket notification for many moderate-risk devices.'],['Implementation','Putting an innovation into real-world use.'],['Barrier to adoption','A factor that makes people less likely to use an innovation.']],mcqs:[q('Which FDA class is highest risk?','Class III','Class I','Class II','Class 0','Risk.'),q('Many Class II devices require:','Premarket notification (510(k))','No review at all','A trademark','A HOSA license','Pathway.'),q('Which is a Class I device example?','Elastic bandage','Pacemaker','Heart valve','Implanted pump','Low risk.'),q('Which career installs and maintains medical equipment?','Biomedical equipment technician','Medical coder','Dietitian','Phlebotomist','Support.'),q('Which is a barrier to adoption?','Workflow disruption','Simple setup','Low cost','Clear instructions','Barrier.'),q('Which training method is common for simple devices?','A short demonstration and quick-start guide','A four-year degree','No training ever','A patent search','Training.'),q('How many points is training requirements worth in Round Two?','5','15','10','20','Rubric.'),q('A pilot means:','Testing in a few facilities before scaling','Flying a plane','Skipping testing','Selling worldwide immediately','Implementation.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe the training requirements, career implications and implementation plan for your medical innovation.',rubric:[['Training',['who','what','how','demonstration','quick-start','competency','minutes']],['Careers',['nurses','assistants','technician','it','therapist','engineer','consumers']],['Regulation',['fda','class','510(k)','pma','risk']],['Implementation',['pilot','manufacture','barriers','workflow','cost','scale','fit']]],model:'Our fall-prevention system would require short, practical training. Night-shift nursing assistants and nurses would learn to place the sensor pad, check batteries, clean the device and respond to silent alerts through a 10-minute hands-on demonstration and a laminated quick-start card, followed by a competency check in which each staff member demonstrates setup correctly; new hires would receive the same training and all staff an annual refresher, taking about 15 minutes per person. Biomedical equipment technicians would need brief training on installation, maintenance and troubleshooting, and IT staff would configure alerts to staff devices. Career implications include nursing assistants and nurses who respond to alerts, nurse managers who set fall-prevention policies, physical therapists who identify high-risk residents, biomedical technicians and IT staff who support the system, and residents and families who use it, while biomedical engineers, product designers, clinical researchers and regulatory affairs specialists would help develop it. Because it is a moderate-risk monitoring device, it would likely be FDA Class II and require premarket notification, which we would research further. Implementation would involve refining the design, safety testing, regulatory clearance, manufacturing, a pilot in two local nursing homes, training staff, evaluating fall rates and scaling up. We would anticipate barriers such as cost, workflow disruption and alarm fatigue and reduce them with simple setup, low cost, silent alerts and no new software, showing that it fits naturally into existing night rounds.'},sources:[RULES,CLASSIFY,BLS_OOH]};

export const MI9={title:'The digital outline and exhibit',hook:'Round One judges see only your digital outline, so it must convince them of your project’s value and make them eager to see the exhibit. At ILC, your exhibit must stand alone—any delegate walking by should understand your innovation without you there. This lesson covers both: the 13-slide outline and an exhibit that fits the rules and stands out.',objectives:['Build each required outline slide within the limits.','Describe the planned exhibit’s creative and artistic impact.','Design an exhibit that fits size, power and safety rules.','Prepare for the Required Project Display Time.'],sections:[
['Required outline slides',[
'The outline is created in **slide style** (any platform: PowerPoint, Canva, Google Slides) and includes: **(a) Title slide**—event name, team member names, HOSA division, chapter number, school name, chartered association (**1 slide**); **(b)** what the innovation is and what it does or how it is used (**up to 4**); **(c)** impact on the future of health care delivery (**up to 2**); **(d)** how it may increase quality of life (**up to 2**); **(e)** how it may reduce health care costs (**up to 2**); **(f)** creative and artistic impact (**up to 2**); and **(g)** reference slides in **APA** style (as many as needed).',
'The total must be **no more than 13 slides**, not counting references. Upload as **one combined PDF** by **May 15 at midnight EST**; links or documents judges cannot access earn no points.',
{t:'table',caption:'Outline structure and Round One points',head:['Section','Max slides','Points'],rows:[['Title slide','1','5'],['What it is and how it is used','4','15'],['Impact on future of health care delivery','2','15'],['Quality of life','2','15'],['Reducing health care costs','2','15'],['Creative and artistic impact','2','15'],['Organization','—','5'],['References','as needed','5'],['Total slides ≤13','—','5'],['Original innovation','—','15']]}]],
['Making the outline persuasive',[
'The outline should give judges a **clear vision** of your exhibit and **convince them of the project’s value**, leaving them wanting to see it in person. Use clear headings, concise bullets, labeled diagrams, prototype photos, charts and short quotes. Keep fonts and colors consistent.',
'Proofread carefully—organization (neat, organized, error-free) is scored. Ask an advisor to review the PDF as if they were a judge with the rubric.',
'Remember that the outline is a **plan**: judges should be able to picture the physical exhibit from it.',
"Use the same visual style in the outline that you plan for the exhibit, such as matching colors and logos. Consistency helps judges connect the outline to the physical exhibit they will see later.",
sort('Strong or weak outline practice?',['Strong','Weak'],[['Labeled diagram of how the device works',0],['Paragraphs of tiny text',1],['Consistent fonts and colors',0],['Spelling errors on the title slide',1],['A photo of the working prototype',0],['A link to a video judges cannot open',1]])]],
['The creative and artistic impact slides',[
'These slides describe **how your final exhibit will look**, **what elements will showcase the innovation** and **what will make it stand out**. Include a sketch or mock-up of the table layout, the backboard design, the prototype’s placement, interactive elements and any battery-powered displays.',
'The top score goes to plans that make it “very evident the exhibit and innovation will stand out” and excite judges to see it in Round Two.',
'Explain your theme, color choices and how design supports understanding.',
chk('What should the creative and artistic impact slides describe?',['How the exhibit will look and what will make it stand out','Only the cost','The team’s favorite foods','The patent claims'],'Plan for the exhibit.')]],
['Exhibit rules',[
'At ILC, exhibits sit on tables (one or two teams per table) within **48 inches wide × 24 inches deep**, including models, electronics, mannequins and all items; there is **no height limit**, but exhibits must be stable and judges must see everything. Items may not hang off the table, and teams may not encroach on neighbors’ space. Exhibits outside these dimensions receive penalty points in Round Two.',
'**No electricity** is provided—use **battery power**. **No Wi-Fi** is provided, and no equipment except tables. Sounds must not disturb neighbors. **Flames, body fluids, living organisms, sharps (even capped or locked) and any invasive procedures or skin puncturing** are prohibited. Write event name, team members, division, chapter number, school and chartered association on the **back** of the exhibit.',
"Measure the exhibit at home on a table marked to 48 by 24 inches with tape. This rehearsal reveals whether models, tablets and signs fit before you travel.",
sort('Exhibit compliant?',['Compliant','Not compliant'],[['Battery-powered tablet playing a looped video',0],['Plug-in monitor',1],['A tall but stable backboard within 48″ × 24″',0],['Items hanging off the table edge',1],['A demo that relies on venue Wi-Fi',1],['Team information on the back',0]])]],
['Designing an exhibit that stands alone',[
'Anyone viewing the exhibit should understand the innovation without a presenter. Organize the exhibit around the **four questions**: what it is and how it is used, impact on health care delivery, quality of life and cost. Use a large, clear **title**, a short **tagline**, labeled **diagrams**, the **prototype** in a central position and perhaps a **battery-powered looped video** demonstrating use.',
'Round Two scores **exhibit visuals**: artistic design (10), creativity and originality (10), overall innovation (10) and safety (5). Apply design principles—contrast, alignment, repetition, proximity and a clear visual hierarchy.',
order('Exhibit layout plan',['Title and tagline at the top of the backboard','Prototype at the center front','“What it is” panel near the prototype','Impact and quality-of-life panels on the sides','Cost panel with a simple chart','Looped battery-powered demo video'])]],
['The Required Project Display Time',[
'All competitors must attend the **Required Project Display Time** at ILC, standing with their innovation and sharing experiences with delegates; missing it costs **15 points** from Round Two. Teams have **15 minutes to assemble** their exhibits before display time, and only registered competitors may set up.',
'Bring spare batteries, tools, tape and backup parts. Prepare a 30-second explanation for visitors and practice demonstrating the prototype safely. Pick up exhibits as instructed—unclaimed exhibits become HOSA property and may be discarded.',
ex('Setup checklist','Plan the 15-minute setup (items invented for practice).',['Minutes 0–5: place backboard and table cover','Minutes 5–9: position prototype and panels','Minutes 9–12: turn on battery devices and test the demo','Minutes 12–15: measure width and depth; check team info on back'],'A timed plan keeps setup within 15 minutes (details invented for practice).')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Outline and exhibit check',{a:{text:'Your outline has 15 slides plus references. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you do?',choices:[['Combine or trim sections to reach 13 or fewer, respecting each section’s maximum','b','Correct.',true],['Submit as is','b','The total-slides item is scored.',false]]},b:{text:'Your exhibit demo needs the internet. What is the fix?',choices:[['Use an offline, battery-powered version of the demo','c','Correct.',true],['Plan to use the venue Wi-Fi','c','No Wi-Fi is provided.',false]]},c:{text:'Your outline and exhibit both meet the rules.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Outline sections: title (1), what it is and how it is used (≤4), delivery impact (≤2), quality of life (≤2), cost reduction (≤2), creative and artistic impact (≤2) and APA references; ≤13 slides excluding references; one PDF by May 15; no inaccessible links.','Make the outline persuasive and organized with diagrams, photos and concise bullets; creative impact slides describe the exhibit’s look and standout elements.','Exhibits: 48″ W × 24″ D (no height limit; stable), battery power only, no Wi-Fi, no flames, body fluids, living organisms, sharps or invasive procedures; team info on the back; stand-alone design; required display time with 15-minute setup (−15 if missed).'],terms:[['Digital outline','A slide-style plan of the exhibit judged in Round One.'],['Stand-alone exhibit','An exhibit viewers can understand without a presenter.'],['Exhibit visuals','Round Two items for artistic design, creativity, overall innovation and safety.']],mcqs:[q('How many slides may describe what the innovation is and how it is used?','Up to 4','Up to 2','1','Up to 13','Section limit.'),q('What is the maximum exhibit width?','48 inches','24 inches','36 inches','72 inches','Dimension.'),q('What is the maximum exhibit depth?','24 inches','48 inches','12 inches','36 inches','Dimension.'),q('Is there a height limit?','No, but exhibits must be stable','Yes, 36 inches','Yes, 12 inches','Yes, 6 feet','Rule.'),q('How must electronic items be powered?','Batteries','Wall outlets','Venue generators','They are not allowed','No electricity provided.'),q('How long do teams have to set up before display time?','15 minutes','5 minutes','1 hour','No time','Setup.'),q('Which item is prohibited even if capped or locked?','Sharps','LEDs','3D-printed parts','Foam models','Safety.'),q('Where should team information be written?','On the back of the exhibit','On the front title','Nowhere','On the floor','Identification.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how your team would build the digital outline and design an exhibit that meets the rules and stands out.',rubric:[['Outline',['title','4','2','13','references','apa','pdf','may 15']],['Persuasive and creative',['diagrams','photos','concise','creative','artistic','stand out','sketch']],['Exhibit rules',['48','24','height','battery','wi-fi','sharps','back']],['Stand-alone and display time',['stand alone','four questions','prototype','video','display time','15 minutes','spare batteries']]],model:'Our digital outline would follow the required structure: one title slide with the event name, our names, division, chapter number, school and chartered association; up to four slides explaining what our fall-prevention system is, its components and step-by-step use with labeled diagrams and prototype photos; two slides each on impact on the future of health care delivery, quality of life and cost reduction; and two creative and artistic impact slides with a sketch of our exhibit layout, theme colors and standout features, followed by APA reference slides. We would keep the total at 13 slides or fewer excluding references, use consistent fonts and colors and concise bullets, proofread carefully, avoid links judges cannot open and have one member upload a single PDF well before May 15. For the exhibit, we would design within 48 inches wide by 24 inches deep, including everything on the table, using a tall but stable backboard with a large title and tagline, the prototype at center front, panels answering the four required questions and a battery-powered tablet looping an offline demo video, since no electricity or Wi-Fi is provided. We would include no flames, body fluids, living organisms, sharps or invasive features, keep sounds quiet and write our team information on the back. Applying contrast, alignment and visual hierarchy, the exhibit would stand alone for any viewer. We would attend the required display time, follow a timed 15-minute setup plan, bring spare batteries and tools and prepare a 30-second explanation for visitors.'},sources:[RULES,UPLOAD]};

export const MI10={title:'The Round Two presentation',hook:'Five minutes, your exhibit and your prototype: that is all you have to teach judges about your innovation, demonstrate it and convince them it belongs in health care. Round Two is worth 130 points, and three content items—explain and teach, demonstration and purpose—are worth 15 points each. A focused, practiced presentation turns months of work into a winning performance.',objectives:['Include all required presentation elements.','Plan a five-minute structure with a clear demonstration.','Use the exhibit and prototype effectively.','Deliver as a coordinated team.'],sections:[
['Required content',[
'The presentation must: **(a)** explain and teach judges about the innovation; **(b)** **demonstrate** the innovation, including how it is used; **(c)** include the **purpose** behind it, why it is needed and how it adds value and benefits the health care system; **(d)** explain **anticipated costs** for the consumer and/or the health care system; **(e)** describe **training requirements**; and **(f)** highlight how it **fits within the health care field** and which practitioners and consumers are needed to implement it.',
'Content scoring: explain and teach (15), demonstration of prototype (15), purpose, value and benefit (15), cost (5), training requirements (5) and career implications (5).',
'Missing any element costs points directly.',
"Build the presentation around the rubric. For each content item, write one or two sentences that clearly address it, then connect those sentences into a natural story.",
match('Required element and example',[['Explain and teach','How the sensor and lights work together'],['Demonstrate','Teammate sits up; lights and alert activate'],['Purpose and value','Nighttime falls are common and costly'],['Cost','About $150 per unit; break-even with one prevented fall'],['Training','10-minute demo and quick-start card'],['Career fit','Nurses, assistants and biomedical technicians']])]],
['Structuring five minutes',[
'A strong structure: **Hook and purpose (about 45 seconds)**—a statistic or story about the need. **Explain the innovation (about 1 minute)**—what it is and how it works, pointing to the exhibit. **Demonstration (about 1 minute 15 seconds)**—show it in use step by step. **Value, cost and training (about 1 minute 15 seconds)**—benefits, costs, training and careers. **Close (about 30 seconds)**—impact on the future and a memorable final line.',
'Time cards are shown at **one minute remaining**, and time is called at five minutes. Plan about **4 minutes 45 seconds**.',
"If your team has four members, consider giving the demonstration to two members—one acting as the user and one narrating—while the other two handle need, value, cost and training.",
ex('Timing plan for three members','Plan a five-minute presentation (one possible split).',['Member 1: hook, need and purpose (0:45)','Member 2: how it works with exhibit (1:00)','Members 2 and 3: live demonstration (1:15)','Member 3: cost, training and careers (1:15)','Member 1: impact and closing line (0:30)','Total: 4:45'],'Every member speaks, with a 15-second margin.')]],
['A convincing demonstration',[
'The demonstration should leave judges feeling **competent about how to use the prototype**. Show the **realistic scenario** (a teammate acting as the user), narrate each step and point out what is happening. Keep it short, rehearsed and safe.',
'Plan for failure: test the prototype immediately before presenting, bring spare batteries and have a **backup** (a short battery-powered video or step-by-step photos) in case of malfunction. If something fails, stay calm and explain what should happen.',
'Presenters may pick up display items and use the space around the exhibit without encroaching on neighbors.',
"Practice the demonstration from where judges will sit. Make sure every step is visible and that teammates do not block the view of the prototype or the exhibit.",
order('Demonstration steps (example)',['Introduce the scenario: a resident at night','Show the sensor pad placed under the mattress','Teammate sits up on the pad','Lights turn on along the path','Show the silent staff alert','Explain how staff respond'])]],
['Explaining value and benefit',[
'Judges score a **clear rationale**: why the innovation is needed (evidence of need), what makes it better than existing options (from your comparison table) and how it adds value to the health care system (safety, efficiency, access, quality of life, cost).',
'Use one or two strong statistics and a short quote from a professional you interviewed. Connect value to the people who benefit—patients, families, staff and facilities.',
'Avoid overclaiming; realistic, evidence-based value is more convincing.',
"End the value section with a single summary sentence that judges can remember, such as “Safer nights, fewer falls and less alarm fatigue for staff.”",
chk('Which best explains value?',['“Unlike loud bed alarms, our system alerts staff silently before residents stand and lights the path, which nurses told us would reduce alarm fatigue.”','“It is cool.”','“It will replace all nurses.”','“Everyone will buy it.”'],'Specific comparison and evidence.')]],
['Delivery and teamwork',[
'Delivery items include **voice**, **stage presence**, **diction**, **team participation**, **organization and flow** and **exhibit incorporated into the presentation**. Speak clearly with varied pace, make eye contact, use purposeful gestures toward the exhibit and avoid fillers.',
'For team participation, each member should speak and carry **equal parts**. Plan smooth handoffs and practice positions around the exhibit so no one blocks it. Use notecards with key phrases; electronic notes are allowed but not shown to judges.',
'Rehearse with a timer, an audience and the actual prototype.',
"Organization and flow are scored, so plan transitions that connect sections logically, such as moving from “why it is needed” to “how it works” to “what it costs.”",
sort('Helps or hurts delivery?',['Helps','Hurts'],[['Gesturing to the prototype while facing judges',0],['Reading notecards word for word',1],['Smooth handoffs between members',0],['One member speaking most of the time',1],['Varying pace for emphasis',0],['Standing in front of the exhibit',1]])]],
['Answering hard questions',[
'Judges may ask follow-up questions about originality, safety, cost, regulation or limitations. Prepare short, honest answers: how you searched for prior art, which FDA class the device might fall under, how you estimated costs and what you would improve next.',
'If you do not know an answer, say so and explain how you would find out. Honesty and clear thinking impress judges more than guesses.',
"Rehearse answers to likely questions as a team and decide in advance who answers which type—for example, the design lead answers technical questions and the business lead answers cost questions.",
match('Likely question and strong answer focus',[['“Is this original?”','Describe your patent and product searches'],['“Is it safe?”','Explain risks and mitigations'],['“What does it cost?”','Give your estimate and assumptions'],['“Does it need FDA review?”','Name the likely class and pathway'],['“What are its limits?”','Acknowledge them and next steps'],['“Who would use it?”','Name practitioners and consumers']])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Presentation day',{a:{text:'During your demo, the lights fail to turn on. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you do?',choices:[['Stay calm, swap the spare battery or switch to your backup video and explain what should happen','b','Correct.',true],['Stop the presentation','b','Have a backup and keep going.',false]]},b:{text:'With one minute remaining, you have not covered training or careers. What do you do?',choices:[['Summarize training and career fit in two clear sentences, then close','c','Correct.',true],['Keep demonstrating','c','All required elements earn points.',false]]},c:{text:'You finish on time with every required element covered.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Required content: explain and teach (15), demonstrate the prototype (15), purpose, value and benefit (15), cost (5), training (5) and career implications (5); Round Two is worth 130 points.','Plan about 4:45: hook and purpose, how it works, demonstration, value, cost, training and careers, and close; time cards at one minute.','Demonstrate a realistic scenario step by step with backups; explain value with evidence and comparisons; deliver with equal team parts, smooth handoffs, notecards and the exhibit incorporated; prepare honest answers to hard questions.'],terms:[['Demonstration','Showing how the prototype works and is used.'],['Value proposition','Why an innovation is better and worth adopting.'],['Backup demonstration','A video or photos used if the prototype fails.']],mcqs:[q('How long is the Round Two presentation?','5 minutes','10 minutes','3 minutes','15 minutes','Time.'),q('Which content item is worth 15 points?','Demonstration of prototype','Cost','Training requirements','Career implications','Rubric.'),q('When are time cards shown?','At one minute remaining','At the start','At three minutes','Never','Timing.'),q('Which must the presentation include?','Training requirements','The judges’ names','The team’s grades','A song','Required element.'),q('If the prototype fails, you should:','Use a backup and explain what should happen','End the presentation','Blame the judges','Skip the demo silently','Preparation.'),q('May presenters pick up display items?','Yes','No','Only judges may','Only after time','Rule.'),q('Team participation requires:','Each member speaking equal parts','One main speaker','The advisor speaking','No speaking','Teamwork.'),q('How many points is Round Two worth?','130','110','240','90','Total.')],frq:{prompt:'Outline a five-minute Round Two presentation for your medical innovation that covers every required element.',rubric:[['Required elements',['explain','demonstrate','purpose','value','cost','training','careers']],['Structure and timing',['hook','4:45','one minute','close','handoffs']],['Demonstration',['scenario','step','prototype','backup','spare','safe']],['Delivery and questions',['equal','eye contact','notecards','exhibit','honest','questions']]],model:'Our three-member team would open with a hook: “Every night, older adults in nursing homes get up in the dark—and many of them fall.” Member 1 would share a CDC statistic on falls and a nurse’s quote to explain the purpose and need in about 45 seconds. Member 2 would teach judges how our system works, pointing to the exhibit’s labeled diagram: a sensor pad under the mattress detects a resident sitting up, soft lights guide the path and staff receive a silent alert. Members 2 and 3 would then demonstrate for about 75 seconds, with one teammate acting as a resident sitting up on the pad while the other narrates each step and shows the lights and the alert on a battery-powered staff device, with spare batteries and a backup video ready. Member 3 would explain value compared with loud bed alarms, our estimated cost of about $150 per unit and how preventing a single fall can cover a facility’s purchase, the 10-minute training with a quick-start card and competency check, and the practitioners involved—nursing assistants, nurses, biomedical technicians, IT staff and physical therapists. Member 1 would close with the future impact on resident safety and independence. We would rehearse to about 4 minutes 45 seconds, give each member equal parts with smooth handoffs, gesture toward the exhibit while facing judges, use notecards only and prepare honest answers about originality, safety, FDA classification and limitations.'},sources:[RULES,FDA]};
